\subsection{Entropy Redistribution}
\label{sec:entropy-redistribution-main}

As a supplementary observation, head pruning also redistributes the attention entropy of surviving heads on DeepSeek ($\Delta\mathcal{H} \in [-0.047, -0.040]$; Appendix~\ref{app:analysis}, Table~\ref{tab:entropy-deltas}). However, entropy redistribution shows model-dependent behavior---the direction reverses on Qwen at $s{=}0.25$ ($\Delta\mathcal{H} = +0.045$)---replicating in only 2/4 Qwen conditions and thus only partially consistent. Importance discrimination collapse remains the more robust, model-independent correlate, though it partially accounts for rather than fully explains the severity divergence.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Implications for deployment.}
A uniform compression budget implicitly accepts order-of-magnitude severity penalties during low-sparsity reasoning phases. Pilot evaluation (Appendix~\ref{app:pilot-mitigation}) achieved 18\% recovery, below the pre-registered 30\% gate, suggesting that effective phase-aware allocation requires finer phase boundary estimation than current proxies provide. Additional mechanisms (e.g., strategy switching, adaptive thresholding) warrant investigation. We expect qualitatively similar behavior for other attention-mass-based scoring methods (H$_2$O \cite{h2o-neurips2023}, SnapKV \cite{snapkv-neurips2024}, PyramidKV \cite{pyramidkv-colm2025}), though quantitative magnitudes may differ. Extended discussion in Appendix~\ref{app:extended-discussion}.

\paragraph{Model scale and architecture.}
All experiments are conducted at the 7--8B parameter scale. Whether the phase-modulated severity patterns persist, amplify, or attenuate at larger scales (e.g., 70B) remains untested. Preliminary cross-architecture validation on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B (LlamaForCausalLM, \S\ref{sec:cross-arch}, Appendix~\ref{app:cross-arch}) partially confirms the qualitative pattern beyond the Qwen2 architecture---monotonically increasing $d_z$ with within-importance phase gradient $\sim$1.0---but with substantially weaker interaction magnitude ($\sim$1/40 of DeepSeek) and mixed statistical evidence (only $s{=}0.30$ significant after Bonferroni correction). The pattern has been tested on two architecture families (Qwen2ForCausalLM, LlamaForCausalLM); comprehensive validation across more diverse architectures (e.g., Mistral, multi-latent attention) and larger scales is needed for full generalization claims.

\paragraph{Task coverage.}
Our primary evaluation uses MATH-500; cross-benchmark validation on GSM8K (\S\ref{sec:cross-benchmark}) confirms the phase gradient direction under importance-based eviction but reveals that random eviction is non-significant on GSM8K (CIs spanning zero), precluding meaningful ratio computation. On MATH-500, strategy specificity is quantifiable ($15$--$17{\times}$); on GSM8K, it manifests as a qualitative gap (significant vs.\ non-significant). This suggests that the phase-strategy interaction is robust in direction but task-dependent in form---complex reasoning (MATH-500) produces quantitative strategy contrast while simpler arithmetic (GSM8K) produces qualitative separation. Qwen GSM8K outputs were truncated at \texttt{max\_new\_tokens}${=}$2047, so Qwen GSM8K absolute values carry this limitation; DeepSeek GSM8K data is untruncated. Our task-complexity explanation for specificity attenuation (lower cognitive load $\to$ more uniform attention $\to$ reduced strategy sensitivity) is supported by within-benchmark correlational evidence but requires validation across a broader range of task complexities; whether different reasoning tasks---code generation, science reasoning, multi-hop QA---produce yet different attenuation profiles remains open. Random eviction on GSM8K exhibits numerical instability (extreme outlier II values), likely a systematic interaction between reduced-precision arithmetic and score-independent eviction.

\paragraph{Symmetric sparsity assumption.}
Our analysis applies symmetric sparsity rates: the same fraction $s$ is used for both head pruning and KV cache eviction. A preliminary spot check under asymmetric allocation (Appendix~\ref{app:asymmetric-robustness}) confirms that the phase gradient is robust to head/KV budget ratios, but systematic characterization of the full asymmetric interaction surface remains future work.

\paragraph{Phase segmentation validation.}
The \texttt{<think>} token validation of sparsity-based phase segmentation (\S\ref{sec:background}, Appendix~\ref{app:think-validation}) is limited to a single model (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B) on a single benchmark (GSM8K, $n{=}48$). Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct does not generate explicit reasoning markers, precluding the same validation. Whether the high agreement ($\kappa{=}0.887$) generalizes to other models with explicit CoT delimiters, or to non-mathematical reasoning tasks, remains open.

\paragraph{Mechanism.}
The importance discrimination collapse mechanism is supported by correlational evidence (consistent Gini/CV reduction across all conditions); causal validation through controlled entropy manipulation is needed. The entropy redistribution component replicates on only 2/4 Qwen conditions, suggesting model-specific factors.

\paragraph{Perplexity vs.\ accuracy.}
Our primary severity metric is perplexity-based (II), chosen because its per-token granularity enables the phase-level decomposition central to our analysis; accuracy is binary per-problem and cannot be meaningfully decomposed by reasoning phase. Accuracy validation on MATH-500 (\S\ref{sec:accuracy-validation}, Appendix~\ref{app:accuracy-details}) shows gradual decline through $s{=}0.25$ ($\delta{=}-4$ to $-13$\,pp) followed by a sharp cliff at $s{=}0.30$ ($\delta{=}-27$\,pp) for Qwen ($n{=}100$, truncation $\leq 4\%$). DeepSeek accuracy deltas are confounded by rising truncation ($16\%$ to $76\%$); non-truncated analysis reveals a dual-regime structure but is limited by small surviving samples at high sparsity. Comprehensive accuracy evaluation across benchmarks remains an important next step.

\paragraph{Sparsity range.}
Our experiments span $s \in \{0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30\}$, sufficient to establish monotonicity (JT test $p \ll 10^{-10}$) but insufficient to characterize the functional form of $G(s)$ or identify potential saturation thresholds. More extreme sparsity ($s > 0.50$) may exhibit qualitatively different interaction dynamics. We do not report inference throughput or latency measurements, which are necessary for a complete deployment analysis but orthogonal to our severity characterization. Extended limitations in Appendix~\ref{app:limitations}.

